Let's evaluate persistent treap performance more concretely. We can implement insert/delete recursively (not split/merge) with arrays left/right/key and priorities precomputed. For toggle, if not present insert; else delete. Standard persistent treap insert:
```
def insert(node, k):
    if node==0: return new_node(k)
    if k < key[node]:
        nl = insert(left[node], k)
        node = clone_with(node, nl, right[node])
        if higher(nl, node):
            node = rotate_right(node) # but node is new root; nl may be new
    else:
        nr = insert(right[node], k)
        node = clone_with(node, left[node], nr)
        if higher(nr, node):
            node = rotate_left(node)
    return node
```
But after rotation, need ensure child relationships and persistence. Suppose we inserted into left child, created `cur` = clone of node with left=nl. If higher(nl,cur), rotate right: new root = clone of nl with right = clone of cur with left = right[nl]? Let's define rotations on indices creating new nodes:
```
def rotate_right(p): # p's left child l has higher priority
    l = left[p]
    # new p' with left = right[l]
    p2 = new_node_copy(key[p], right[l], right[p])? Actually p2 left=right[l], right=right[p]
    l2 = new_node_copy(key[l], left[l], p2)
    return l2
```
